## Code Review (GPT-5.5)
Request changes 🔴 because the same readOnly hidden-input autofill path still exists in NumberField, and the new regression coverage can be much smaller.

### 1. Bugs / Issues

#### 1. 🔴 NumberField still accepts hidden-input autofill while readOnly (blocking)

The PR fixes Select and AriaCombobox, but NumberField has the same supported hidden-input autofill path and still only guards `defaultPrevented`. By code reading, a `readOnly` NumberField can still update through this hidden input because the input does not receive `readOnly`, and the handler does not check the root locked state.

`packages/react/src/number-field/root/NumberFieldRoot.tsx`

```tsx
onChange(event) {
  // Workaround for https://github.com/facebook/react/issues/9023
  if (event.nativeEvent.defaultPrevented) {
    return;
  }

  // Handle browser autofill.
  const nextValue = event.currentTarget.valueAsNumber;
  const parsedValue = Number.isNaN(nextValue) ? null : nextValue;

  setDirty(parsedValue !== validityData.initialValue);
  setValue(parsedValue, details);
}
```

This leaves the same class of bug open in another public component that already has a hidden-input browser autofill test.

**Fix:** Apply the same locked-state guard to `NumberFieldRoot` and add a regression for at least `readOnly` hidden-input autofill; include `event.preventBaseUIHandler?.()` there too when the handler is wrapped by `Field.Root`.

#### 2. 🟡 The locked-state regression tests can be table-driven (non-blocking)

The new tests repeat the same matrix several times: `readOnly` vs `disabled`, inside `Field.Root` vs outside `Field.Root`, then the same hidden-input change and “no public state changed” assertions. That makes this bug fix look larger than it is.

`packages/react/src/select/root/SelectRoot.test.tsx`

```tsx
it('ignores hidden-input autofill when readOnly', async () => { ... });
it('ignores hidden-input autofill when disabled', async () => { ... });
it('ignores hidden-input autofill when readOnly outside Field', async () => { ... });
it('ignores hidden-input autofill when disabled outside Field', async () => { ... });
```

**Recommendation:** Extract small render/assert helpers per component and drive the lock state with `it.each(['readOnly', 'disabled'] as const)`. For Select and Combobox, a second table dimension for `withField` would keep the Field error assertion where it matters while removing most of the duplicated JSX.

### 2. Simplification Opportunities

The production change is already close to minimal for Select and AriaCombobox. If NumberField is included too, a tiny shared predicate may be worth it, for example “ignore this hidden input change when the native event was already prevented or the root is locked, and prevent the Field validation handler when possible.” I would only add that helper if it removes duplication across Select, AriaCombobox, and NumberField; otherwise the source-level inline guard is fine.

## Codex Review (GPT-5.5)
Reviewed the full PR branch against freshly fetched `upstream/master`; dominant type is `bug-fix` with secondary `tests`, `a11y`, and bundle-size impact. The hover-specific fix is well covered, but keyboard focus and delayed-open timing still leave branch-relevant regressions.

### 1. Bugs / Issues

#### 1. 🔴 Inner trigger focus can still open the outer tooltip (blocking)

`packages/react/src/tooltip/trigger/TooltipTrigger.tsx`

```tsx
const focusProps = useFocus(floatingRootContext, { enabled: !disabled }).reference;

// ...

props: [
  hoverProps,
  focusProps,
  rootTriggerProps,
  {
    onMouseOver(event: React.MouseEvent) {
      handleNestedTriggerHover(event.nativeEvent);
    },
```

The nested-trigger guard only covers hover. React `onFocus` bubbles, so focusing an enabled inner `Tooltip.Trigger` inside an outer trigger can run both focus handlers and open both ancestor and inner tooltips for keyboard users.

**Fix:** add the nested-trigger veto to focus opens as well, either through a `useFocus` `shouldOpen` guard or an internal `onFocus` handler that calls `event.preventBaseUIHandler()` when the focus target is an enabled descendant tooltip trigger. Add a regression test for focusing the uncontrolled inner trigger.

#### 2. 🟡 A stale parent hover timer can open too early after returning from the nested trigger (non-blocking)

`packages/react/src/floating-ui-react/hooks/useHoverReferenceInteraction.ts`

```ts
instance.openChangeTimeout.start(openDelay, () => {
  if (shouldOpen && checkShouldOpen()) {
    store.setOpen(true, createChangeEventDetails(REASONS.triggerHover, event, triggerNode));
  }
});
```

If the pointer starts over the outer area, moves onto the inner trigger before the outer delay expires, then moves back to the parent area before the original timer fires, `shouldOpen()` becomes true again and the old timer can open the parent earlier than a fresh parent-area delay.

**Fix:** cancel or invalidate pending hover/rest opens when nested hover starts, or tie delayed callbacks to an epoch. Add a test for outer hover -> half delay -> inner hover -> parent-area return, asserting the parent waits a full new delay.

#### 3. 🟡 Two regression tests do not prove the edge cases their names claim (non-blocking)

`packages/react/src/tooltip/root/TooltipRoot.test.tsx`

```tsx
// Disabled nested trigger case only hovers the outer trigger.
const outerTrigger = screen.getByTestId('outer-trigger');

fireEvent.pointerDown(outerTrigger, { pointerType: 'mouse' });
fireEvent.pointerEnter(outerTrigger, { clientX: 10, clientY: 10 });
```

The disabled nested-trigger test never hovers the disabled inner trigger, so it would pass even if disabled nested triggers were still treated as enabled.

```tsx
const innerShadowTarget = document.createElement('span');
innerShadowRoot.appendChild(innerShadowTarget);

innerShadowRoot.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent('mouseover', { bubbles: true, composed: true }));
```

The shadow-root test dispatches from the `ShadowRoot`, not `innerShadowTarget`, so it does not really prove traversal from an element inside the shadow tree back to the host trigger.

**Fix:** hover the disabled inner trigger in the disabled case, and dispatch the composed shadow event from `innerShadowTarget`.

### Performance Considerations

I ran `pnpm size:snapshot` on the PR head and on the PR merge-base (`53c3d1e`) to isolate the branch delta.

| Entry | Parsed Δ | Gzip Δ |
|---|---:|---:|
| `@base-ui/react/tooltip` | +1,197 B | +449 B |
| `@base-ui/react` | +1,192 B | +481 B |
| `@base-ui/react/internals/shadowDom` | +220 B | +78 B |
| Shared hover users such as `menu`, `popover`, `preview-card` | +73 B each | +32-40 B each |

Best bundle-size reductions to try:

1. Only mark enabled triggers with `data-base-ui-tooltip-trigger`, then simplify the selector to `[data-base-ui-tooltip-trigger]`.
2. Replace the shared exported `closest()` helper with a tooltip-local composed-path/host scan that checks the marker directly; this should remove the `shadowDom` entrypoint growth and the extra re-export cost.
3. Compute `event.composedPath()` once in `TooltipTrigger` and drop the separate `getTarget` import for this path.

### Test Coverage Assessment

`pnpm test:jsdom TooltipRoot --no-watch` passed on the PR head. Coverage is broad for pointer hover, detached handles, disabled triggers, shadow DOM, and zero-delay providers, but it needs the focus regression and stale-delay regression above before this is safe to merge.

### Recommendation

Request changes 🔴

The pointer-hover fix is directionally solid, but the keyboard focus path can still produce nested ancestor tooltips, which is an accessibility regression. I’d also address the stale timer before merge because it preserves the old flicker/early-open class of bug in a fast pointer path.

Yeah good question. I imagine it should work like this:
```js
// stops the entire handler from having an effect
// event.stopPropagation() isn't called anyway
event.preventBaseUIHandler();

// *only* prevents event.stopPropagation() from being called
// but the logic still executes
event.preventBaseUIHandler({
  preventStopPropagation: true,
});
```

## Summary
Request to add `onPointerDownOutside` prop to the Dialog component to allow developers to customize the behavior when users click outside the dialog popup. This would enable preventing dialog closure for specific DOM elements or implementing custom logic before the dialog closes.

## Examples in other libraries
- **Radix UI Dialog**: [`onPointerDownOutside` prop](https://www.radix-ui.com/primitives/docs/components/dialog#content) - Allows preventing default behavior and customizing outside click handling
- **Headless UI Dialog**: Similar functionality through event handlers
- **React Aria Dialog**: Provides `onDismiss` with event details

Example from Radix UI:
```jsx
<Dialog.Content
  onPointerDownOutside={(event) => {
    // Prevent dialog from closing when clicking on elements with specific class
    if (event.target.closest('.prevent-popup-close')) {
      event.preventDefault();
    }
  }}
>
  {/* Dialog content */}
</Dialog.Content>
```

## Motivation
This feature is needed for several common use cases:

1. **Preventing accidental closure**: When the dialog contains interactive elements that extend outside the dialog bounds (like dropdowns, tooltips, or floating menus), users might accidentally click on these elements and close the dialog unexpectedly.

2. **Complex UI interactions**: In applications with overlapping UI elements or multi-step forms, developers need fine-grained control over when dialogs should close.

3. **Accessibility considerations**: Some users with motor disabilities might accidentally click outside the dialog and lose their progress.

4. **Custom validation**: Developers might want to show confirmation dialogs or validate unsaved changes before allowing the dialog to close.

5. **Design system consistency**: Teams migrating from other libraries (like Radix UI) expect this level of control for consistent user experience.

## Proposed API
```jsx
<Dialog.Popup
  onPointerDownOutside={(event) => {
    // Custom logic here
    // Call event.preventDefault() to prevent default closing behavior
  }}
>
  {/* Dialog content */}
</Dialog.Popup>
```

The event object should include:
- `event.target` - The element that was clicked
- `event.preventDefault()` - Method to prevent the default closing behavior
- Standard pointer event properties

## Additional considerations
- This should work alongside the existing `dismissible` prop
- The callback should be called before the dialog's close logic
- Should maintain consistency with other Base UI components that might have similar outside interaction patterns

This enhancement would significantly improve the flexibility of the Dialog component while maintaining backward compatibility.
